<%
# ==============================================================================
#  Texl sample document
#
#  A tour of the Swan language and of the texl engine. Each example line shows
#  an expression on the left and, after the arrow, the tag that evaluates it.
#
#  Render it with:   cargo run -p texl-cli -- texl-cli/tests/fixtures/sample.texl
#  Expected output:  sample.expected.txt, next to this file
# ==============================================================================
title = "Texl Sample Document",
reader = "Ada"
%><% title %>
====================

Text outside the tags is copied as it is. Each tag is replaced by the value of
its expression. Tags run in order and share one scope, so names assigned in a
tag (like `title`, in the first tag of this file) can be used by later tags.


1. Literals and types
---------------------

  42                       -> <% 42 %>
  3.14                     -> <% 3.14 %>
  -0.5                     -> <% -0.5 %>
  "hello"                  -> <% "hello" %>
  TRUE                     -> <% TRUE %>
  ()                       -> [<% () %>]   (Nothing renders as empty text)
  type 42                  -> <% type 42 %>
  type "hello"             -> <% type "hello" %>
  type [1, 2]              -> <% type [1, 2] %>
  type {a: 1}              -> <% type {a: 1} %>
  type (x -> x)            -> <% type (x -> x) %>


2. Arithmetic
-------------

  2 + 3 * 4                -> <% 2 + 3 * 4 %>
  (2 + 3) * 4              -> <% (2 + 3) * 4 %>
  2 ^ 10                   -> <% 2 ^ 10 %>
  7 / 2                    -> <% 7 / 2 %>
  7 % 3                    -> <% 7 % 3 %>
  "Te" + "xl"              -> <% "Te" + "xl" %>


3. Comparison and logic
-----------------------

  3 < 5                    -> <% 3 < 5 %>
  "abc" < "abd"            -> <% "abc" < "abd" %>
  [1, 2] == [1, 2]         -> <% [1, 2] == [1, 2] %>
  !0                       -> <% !0 %>
  TRUE & "hello"           -> <% TRUE & "hello" %>
  0 | "world"              -> <% 0 | "world" %>
  TRUE ? "yes"             -> <% TRUE ? "yes" %>
  FALSE ? "yes"            -> [<% FALSE ? "yes" %>]
  () ; "fallback"          -> <% () ; "fallback" %>
  FALSE ; "fallback"       -> <% FALSE ; "fallback" %>
  5 > 3 ? "big" ; "small"  -> <% 5 > 3 ? "big" ; "small" %>


4. Variables
------------

Let price = 12.5 and qty = 4<% price = 12.5, qty = 4 %>

  price * qty              -> <% price * qty %>
  x : 7                    -> <% x : 7 %>   (`:` binds and returns the value)
  x = 7                    -> [<% x = 7 %>]   (`=` binds and returns Nothing)
  (a, b) = (1, 2), (b, a)  -> <% (a, b) = (1, 2), (b, a) %>
  y = 10, z = 5, y + z     -> <% y = 10, z = 5, y + z %>
  label "w" 99             -> <% label "w" 99 %>
  w                        -> <% w %>


5. Tuples
---------

  1, 2, "three"            -> <% 1, 2, "three" %>
  (1, (), (2, 3))          -> <% (1, (), (2, 3)) %>
  (10, 20) + (1, 2)        -> <% (10, 20) + (1, 2) %>
  (1, 2) == (1, 2)         -> <% (1, 2) == (1, 2) %>


6. Lists
--------

Let nums = [10, 20, 30]<% nums = [10, 20, 30] %>

  nums                     -> <% nums %>
  nums 1                   -> <% nums 1 %>
  nums 10                  -> [<% nums 10 %>]
  list.size nums           -> <% list.size nums %>
  list.enum nums           -> <% list.enum nums %>
  list.enum (nums + [40])  -> <% list.enum (nums + [40]) %>
  list.reverse nums        -> <% list.enum (list.reverse nums) %>   (via list.enum)
  list.head 2 nums         -> <% list.enum (list.head 2 nums) %>   (via list.enum)
  list.tail 2 nums         -> <% list.enum (list.tail 2 nums) %>   (via list.enum)
  list.find 20 nums        -> <% list.find 20 nums %>
  list.find 99 nums        -> <% list.find 99 nums %>


7. Namespaces
-------------

Let point = {x: 3, y: 4}<% point = {x: 3, y: 4} %>

  point                    -> <% point %>
  point.x                  -> <% point.x %>
  point.(x * y)            -> <% point.(x * y) %>
  point "y"                -> <% point "y" %>
  nspace.enum point        -> <% nspace.enum point %>
  nspace.size point        -> <% nspace.size point %>


8. Functions
------------

Let double = x -> x * 2<% double = x -> x * 2 %>
Let add = (a, b) -> a + b<% add = (a, b) -> a + b %>
Let fact = n -> n <= 1 ? 1 ; n * fact(n - 1)<% fact = n -> n <= 1 ? 1 ; n * fact(n - 1) %>
Let grade = s -> s >= 90 ? "A" ; s >= 80 ? "B" ; "C"<% grade = s -> s >= 90 ? "A" ; s >= 80 ? "B" ; "C" %>

  double                   -> <% double %>
  double 21                -> <% double 21 %>
  double 2 + 3             -> <% double 2 + 3 %>
  add(3, 4)                -> <% add(3, 4) %>
  fact 10                  -> <% fact 10 %>
  (95, 85, 70) => grade    -> <% (95, 85, 70) => grade %>
  (1, 2, 3) => double      -> <% (1, 2, 3) => double %>
  (double >> double) 5     -> <% (double >> double) 5 %>
  (math.sqrt << double) 8  -> <% (math.sqrt << double) 8 %>

Currying and closures:<% inc = (a -> b -> a + b) 1, scale = factor -> (v -> v * factor), triple = scale 3 %>

  inc = (a -> b -> a + b) 1        inc 10    -> <% inc 10 %>
  triple = scale 3                 triple 5  -> <% triple 5 %>


9. Built-in namespaces
----------------------

  text.size "hello"              -> <% text.size "hello" %>
  text.upper "texl"              -> <% text.upper "texl" %>
  text.lower "TEXL"              -> <% text.lower "TEXL" %>
  text.trim "  padded  "         -> [<% text.trim "  padded  " %>]
  text.find "l" "hello"          -> <% text.find "l" "hello" %>
  text.rfind "l" "hello"         -> <% text.rfind "l" "hello" %>
  text.head 3 "swanky"           -> <% text.head 3 "swanky" %>
  text.tail 4 "swanky"           -> <% text.tail 4 "swanky" %>
  text.split "," "a,b,c"         -> <% text.split "," "a,b,c" %>
  text.join "-" ("a", "b", "c")  -> <% text.join "-" ("a", "b", "c") %>
  text.enum "abc"                -> <% text.enum "abc" %>
  text.from 42 + "!"             -> <% text.from 42 + "!" %>
  numb.from "41" + 1             -> <% numb.from "41" + 1 %>
  bool.from ""                   -> <% bool.from "" %>
  bool.any (0, 1)                -> <% bool.any (0, 1) %>
  bool.all (0, 1)                -> <% bool.all (0, 1) %>
  math.PI                        -> <% math.PI %>
  math.round (math.PI * 100)     -> <% math.round (math.PI * 100) %>
  math.pow 10 2                  -> <% math.pow 10 2 %>   (2 ^ 10)
  math.max (1, 5, 2)             -> <% math.max (1, 5, 2) %>
  (4, 9, 16) => math.sqrt        -> <% (4, 9, 16) => math.sqrt %>
  time.to_string 1780000000      -> <% time.to_string 1780000000 %>
  time.from_string "2026-05-28"  -> <% time.from_string "2026-05-28" %>
  time.week_day 1780000000       -> <% time.week_day 1780000000 %>   (0 is Sunday)
  time.week_number 1780000000    -> <% time.week_number 1780000000 %>
  (time.to_date 0).year          -> <% (time.to_date 0).year %>
  (parse "x + 1") {x: 41}        -> <% (parse "x + 1") {x: 41} %>


10. Undefined values
--------------------

Invalid operations do not stop the rendering: they produce an Undefined value,
which spreads through the expressions that use it.

  "hello" - 1                    -> <% "hello" - 1 %>
  ("hello" - 1) + 5              -> <% ("hello" - 1) + 5 %>
  !("hello" - 1)                 -> <% !("hello" - 1) %>   (Undefined is falsy)
  type ("hello" - 1)             -> <% type ("hello" - 1) %>

Let err = undef.inspect("hello" - 1)<% err = undef.inspect("hello" - 1) %>

  err.type                       -> <% err.type %>
  err.op                         -> <% err.op %>
  err.(lhv, rhv)                 -> <% err.(lhv, rhv) %>


11. Promises
------------

A tag that returns a promise makes the rendering wait until it resolves.

  p = promise.create(),
  doubled = p ~> double,
  promise.resolve p 21,
  doubled                        -> <% p = promise.create(), doubled = p ~> double, promise.resolve p 21, doubled %>


12. Imports
-----------

`texl.import(uri)` returns a promise of the document namespace
{uri, source, data, rendering}. Relative URIs resolve against the folder of
the importing document.

<% texl.import("partials/greeting.texl") ~> (doc -> doc.rendering) %>
The definitions of an imported document are in its `data`. Here they are
bound to the global name `fmt`:<% texl.import("lib/format.texl") ~> (doc -> bind "fmt" doc.data) %>

  fmt.plural(1, "apple")         -> <% fmt.plural(1, "apple") %>
  fmt.plural(3, "apple")         -> <% fmt.plural(3, "apple") %>
  fmt.pad 12 "Total"             -> <% fmt.pad 12 "Total" %>
  fmt.price (price * qty / 3)    -> <% fmt.price (price * qty / 3) %>

Fields of an imported document:

  nspace.enum doc                -> <% texl.import("lib/format.texl") ~> (doc -> nspace.enum doc) %>
  fmt.basename doc.uri           -> <% texl.import("lib/format.texl") ~> (doc -> fmt.basename doc.uri) %>
  text.size doc.source > 0       -> <% texl.import("lib/format.texl") ~> (doc -> text.size doc.source > 0) %>

Each document is loaded once: later imports, even through a different relative
path, share the first result.

  <% texl.import("partials/counted.texl") ~> (doc -> text.trim_tail doc.rendering) %>
  <% texl.import("./partials/../partials/counted.texl") ~> (doc -> text.trim_tail doc.rendering) %>
  load_count                     -> <% load_count %>

A document that cannot be loaded gives Nothing:

  missing.texl                   -> <% texl.import("missing.texl") ~> (doc -> doc ; "(not found)") %>


13. How values render
---------------------

  [1, 2, 3]                      -> <% [1, 2, 3] %>
  {a: 1, b: 2}                   -> <% {a: 1, b: 2} %>
  x -> x                         -> <% x -> x %>
  "a" - 1                        -> <% "a" - 1 %>
  (1, "two", TRUE)               -> <% (1, "two", TRUE) %>
